1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,990
JESS: How are you getting on with your art project, Tom?

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:09,190
TOM: OK. Like, they gave us the theme of birds to base our project on,

3
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:12,310
and I'm not really all that interested in wildlife.

4
00:00:12,310 --> 00:00:14,550
But I'm starting to get into it.

5
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:17,420
I've pretty well finished the introductory stage.

6
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:22,260
JESS: So have I. When they gave us all those handouts with details of books

7
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,763
and websites to look at,

8
00:00:23,763 --> 00:00:28,520
I was really put off, but the more I read, the more interested I got.

9
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,360
TOM: Me too. I found I could research

10
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:36,410
so many different aspects of birds in art - colour, movement, texture.

11
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:39,990
So I was looking forward to the Bird Park visit.

12
00:00:39,990 --> 00:00:45,610
JESS: What a letdown! It poured with rain and we hardly saw a single bird.

13
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:49,530
Much less use than the trip to the Natural History Museum.

14
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:53,100
TOM: Yeah. I liked all the stuff about evolution there.

15
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:58,910
The workshop sessions with Dr Fletcher were good too, especially the brainstorming sessions.

16
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:01,860
JESS: I missed those because I was ill

17
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:05,730
I wish we could've seen the projects last year's students did.

18
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:09,920
TOM: Mm. I suppose they want us to do our own thing, not copy.

19
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:12,420
JESS: Have you drafted your proposal yet?

20
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:14,960
TOM: Yes, but I haven't handed it in.

21
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,630
I need to amend some parts.

22
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:22,903
I've realised the notes from my research are almost all just descriptions,

23
00:01:22,903 --> 00:01:25,100
I haven't actually evaluated anything.

24
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:26,850
So I'll have to fix that.

25
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:29,690
JESS: Oh, I didn't know we had to do that.

26
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:31,650
I'll have to look at that too.

27
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:34,830
Did you do a timeline for the project?

28
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:36,800
TOM: Yes, and a mind map.

29
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,220
JESS: Yeah, so did I. I quite enjoyed that.

30
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:45,000
But it was hard having to explain the basis for my decisions in my action plan.

31
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:45,910
TOM: What?

32
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:47,770
JESS: You know, give a rationale.

33
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:50,020
TOM: I didn't realise we had to do that.

34
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,000
OK, I can add it now.

35
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,940
And I've done the video diary presentation

36
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:00,510
and worked out what I want my outcome to be in the project.

37
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:06,022
JESS: Someone told me it's best not to be too precise about your actual outcome at this stage,

38
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:09,390
so you have more scope to explore your ideas later on.

39
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:14,040
So I'm going to go back to my proposal to make it a bit more vague.

40
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:17,470
TOM: Really? OK, I'll change that too then.

41
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:23,180
TOM: One part of the project I'm unsure about is

42
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,228
where we choose some paintings of birds

43
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:27,010
and say what they mean to us.

44
00:02:27,010 --> 00:02:30,720
Like, I chose a painting of a falcon by Landseer.

45
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:35,695
I like it because the bird's standing there with his head turned to one side,

46
00:02:35,695 --> 00:02:38,680
but he seems to be staring straight at you.

47
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,760
But I can't just say it's a bit scary, can I?

48
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:48,920
JESS: You could talk about the possible danger suggested by the bird's look.

49
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:50,320
TOM: Oh, OK.

50
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:54,870
JESS: There's a picture of a fish hawk by Audubon I like.

51
00:02:54,870 --> 00:02:58,935
It's swooping over the water with a fish in its talons,

52
00:02:58,935 --> 00:03:03,370
and with great black wings which take up most of the picture.

53
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:07,730
TOM: So you could discuss it in relation to predators and food chains?

54
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:13,140
JESS: Well actually I think I'll concentrate on the impression of rapid motion it gives.

55
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:14,160
TOM: Right.

56
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,840
JESS: Do you know that picture of a kingfisher by van Gogh

57
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:20,890
it's perching on a reed growing near a stream.

58
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:25,420
TOM: Yes it's got these beautiful blue and red and black shades.

59
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:27,980
JESS: Mm hm. I've actually chosen it

60
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:31,726
because I saw a real kingfisher once when I was little,

61
00:03:31,726 --> 00:03:35,930
I was out walking with my grandfather, and I've never forgotten it.

62
00:03:35,930 --> 00:03:38,760
TOM: So we can use a personal link?

63
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:39,320
JESS: Sure.

64
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:42,614
TOM: OK. There's a portrait called William Wells,

65
00:03:42,614 --> 00:03:48,790
I can't remember the artist but it's a middle-aged man who's just shot a bird.

66
00:03:48,790 --> 00:03:56,100
And his expression, and the way he's holding the bird in his hand suggests he's not sure about what he's done.

67
00:03:56,100 --> 00:04:02,820
To me it's about how ambiguous people are in the way they exploit the natural world.

68
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:06,950
JESS: Interesting. There's Gauguin's picture Vairumati.

69
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:09,240
He did it in Tahiti.

70
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:14,576
It's a woman with a white bird behind her that is eating a lizard,

71
00:04:14,576 --> 00:04:19,150
and what I'm interested in is what idea this bird refers to.

72
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:23,720
Apparently, it's a reference to the never-ending cycle of existence.

73
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:29,110
TOM: Wow. I chose a portrait of a little boy, Giovanni de Medici.

74
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:32,380
He's holding a tiny bird in one fist.

75
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:36,680
I like the way he's holding it carefully so he doesn't hurt it.

76
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,000
JESS: Ah right.
